// 24_strmin.voi — a MISSING argument to a str native is refused, loudly.
//
// The mirror of 19_strarity, and the more dangerous half. A surplus argument
// was silently ignored; a missing one was read anyway: `"hello".substr()`
// reached `I(argv[1])` and dereferenced past the end of the caller's argv —
// SIGSEGV, with nothing to catch and nothing to read.
//
// voilac/chk/native.voi now refuses every NAMED spelling of that call at
// compile time (tests/negative/nativearity.voi), but a METHOD call is a call
// on an ordinary expression whose type is what would decide — expression
// typing, which does not exist yet — so the method spelling is the one that
// reaches str_arity[] in runtime/src/str.c. This file is the proof that it
// still does.
//
// The abort is the point of the test, so it is the LAST thing main does.
package main
use "std/str"

func main() {
    // The optional trailing argument is real, and both forms must keep
    // working: a minimum that rejected `substr(s, 2)` would be a worse bug
    // than the crash.
    let s = "hello"
    say s.substr(2)
    say s.substr(2, 3)
    say s.find_any("l")
    say s.find_any("l", 4)

    // ...and the missing one aborts instead of reading wild memory.
    say s.substr()
    say "NOT REACHED"
}
